# `index.html` — the main file

One self-contained page: ~29,000 lines, ~2.3 MB, ~60 views, the "Instrument" system. No
build step, no external runtime dependencies. Open it in a browser to run it.

## How to work in it

- **Never read the whole file.** Read the region you need. Locate first with `grep -n`,
  then read a bounded range.
- **Never pass replacement code through the shell.** Quotes get stripped silently and a
  syntax check still passes, so the damage surfaces later and far from its cause. Write
  the new text to a file, splice it in with a Node script, and **assert on unique anchor
  strings** — the script should refuse to write if an anchor matches zero times or more
  than once.
- **Scope every new CSS rule under `#fxRoot`** (or the owning view's id). `.dw-hd`,
  `.dw-tabs`, `.dw-body` and `.dw-foot` are shared class names between the Flux review
  workspace and the GL ledger inspector (`.acctsurf .gl-detail`); an unscoped selector
  restyles the other one.
- **Verify in the browser, not by reading.** Drive the view, assert the DOM, check the
  console. Geometry in this file is *measured* at runtime (`sizeDrawer`, `syncStick`), so
  reasoning about the CSS alone will mislead you.

## Structural facts worth knowing before you edit

- `<script id="egl-data">` is a **baked data snapshot**, not an import. It is serialised
  by `packages/core/tools/emit_enterprise_gl.mjs` and read by exactly one view,
  `view-egl`. This is the only bridge between `packages/` and the prototypes, and it is
  build-time and one-directional.
- The Flux review workspace renders at **two densities from one set of renderers**:
  `dwCtx()` derives every figure once per paint, `RWSEC` is a registry of named parts, and
  `RW_LAYOUT` is a pure composition table that *is* the entire mode flag. A part absent
  from `RWSEC` cannot render in either density. Do not fork it into two builders.
  Any refactor there must pass the byte-identity probe: hash `#fxDwBody.innerHTML` for
  every tab on every line before and after; they must be identical strings.
- A **materiality threshold** (`r.req`) decides whether a line must be explained at all. A
  **tolerance** (`r.clear`) decides whether an explanation covers enough of the movement.
  They are different numbers — conflating them is how a line below the threshold ends up
  permanently unsubmittable.
- Roles resolve at call time from `USER_ROLE` through `FX_CAPS`, keyed on
  `Accounting Manager` / `Controller` / `Chief Accounting Officer` / `Asset Manager` /
  `FP&A Analyst` / `External Auditor`. An unknown role falls back to view-only.
  Segregation of duties is enforced at the action (`canReviewLine`), not in the UI.

## Checks

```bash
node tools/check_chrome_themes.mjs
```

Then the browser sweep: open the file, drive every tab, assert each view rendered, console
clean.
